=========================== Release 0.4.1 ==============================

2026-10-03  Yury Kirsanov
        * [upgrade] State carries over from 0.4.0, the cluster wire does
          not. Stop every node of a fleet, then start them all on 0.4.1; a
          node-by-node roll leaves 0.4.0 and 0.4.1 nodes unable to hear each
          other.

        * [cluster] A cluster has an id, and two clusters never merge. The
          node that founds a cluster mints a UUID (v7) and every member keeps
          it in its state directory; every cluster datagram carries it in
          its authenticated header, and a node ignores any other cluster's
          frames and says so. [cluster] cluster_id pins it (a name or a
          UUID), so staging and production cannot meet even on one group.

        * [cluster] [cluster] multicast_ttl lets the cluster group cross a
          multicast router (default 1, one segment).

        * [cluster] The member limit is one consistent 256 everywhere, and a
          full fleet refuses a new node out loud instead of ignoring it;
          [cluster] max_members sets a lower limit.

        * [eager] A miss on an eager collection is answered at once - no
          node asks the fleet for a key every node would hold. A node still
          asks while it is recovering, for 30 s after it starts or a peer
          returns, and after it has refused a copy for lack of memory
          (/stats cluster.eager_miss_final). A value over 58,000 bytes
          reaches the other nodes by the repair sweep, ~10-20 s after the
          write. The JSON mset verb now replicates its keys at once.

        * [core] A KEYS walk takes a time budget per worker turn
          ([daemon] keys_turn_us, default 250 us) rather than a fixed
          1,024 buckets, so a sparse table no longer costs one turn per
          1,024 empty buckets.

        * [ops] A node waits up to [daemon] address_wait_s (30 s) for a
          configured address to appear instead of failing its first bind;
          two daemons can no longer share a state or WAL directory; the
          members card says why a member left (goodbye, silent, forgotten);
          /stats, the page and /metrics say whether the rebalancer has
          anything to do; the WAL card's out-of-order count is labelled as
          the harmless figure it is.

=========================== Release 0.4.0 ==============================

2026-09-30  Yury Kirsanov
        * [upgrade] There is no upgrade path from 0.3.x. The write-ahead log,
          the snapshot and the cluster wire changed in this line: 0.4.0 does
          not take over a 0.3.x node's state or join a 0.3.x fleet, and
          perfdump/perfload do not carry data across either. Deploy 0.4.0 with
          an empty [wal] dir and snapshot directory and let the application
          re-populate its keys.

        * [security] The slow log stored the RESP password. An AUTH, or a
          HELLO carrying AUTH, slower than slowlog_usec kept its arguments in
          SLOWLOG GET, /stats and the status page; they are now stored as
          (redacted). Affected: every release before 0.4.0. If a node's slow
          log may hold an AUTH, run SLOWLOG RESET and consider rotating the
          [secrets] resp password.

        * [security] The status page could run what a key carried. Every
          cache-fed string the page renders is escaped, and the page carries a
          Content-Security-Policy that pins its one script by hash, so an
          injected script does not run even if an escape is missed. Every HTTP
          answer carries nosniff, no-referrer and no-framing headers.
          Affected: every release before 0.4.0. Keep the HTTP door on a
          trusted network either way (http_allow, [secrets] http).

        * [security] A PSUBSCRIBE pattern could stall a node or read past its
          buffer (exponential matching on '*'; an unclosed '[set]'). The
          pattern matcher is replaced. Affected: pre-release candidates of
          this line only; no earlier release has pub/sub.

        * [pubsub] Redis pub/sub on every door and across the fleet:
          SUBSCRIBE, PSUBSCRIBE, PUBLISH and PUBSUB with Redis's rules and
          frames, the same verbs on the native doors and in libperfd. A
          publish on any node reaches the subscribers on every node over a
          relay port, optionally only the nodes that want it; pushes over UDP
          for native subscribers; keyspace notifications (notify_events).

        * [core] SET ... NX/XX everywhere: in a cluster one node decides each
          key, so two callers cannot both win an NX lock; SETNX and SETXX on
          the native doors and perfd_set_cond() in libperfd.

        * [json] JSON documents are a type: GET on a document and JSON.GET on
          a string answer as Redis 8 does, path updates keep the type, and
          redis_types = strict | loose chooses per collection.

        * [build] Two editions from one tree: 'make menuconfig' builds the
          standalone cache - no cluster code, a third smaller - or the
          clustered daemon.

        * [wal] A node that loses WAL records heals itself instead of failing:
          HEALING, a snapshot, READY again, every acknowledged write kept;
          /stats wal.unhealed says what is still owed.

        * [cluster] Restarts and outages: a returning node reconciles what it
          replayed against a peer that stayed up, narrowed by slot digests;
          cluster.restart says whether restarting a node now would be
          witnessed; a privileged 'fleetstop' stops a fleet with clean
          snapshots.

        * [cluster] A fleet that cannot be confused by its own members: a
          cluster wire epoch refuses a peer that would mis-execute frames, a
          refused node can no longer take mastership, and a forwarded request
          tells the client it is behind - a hint on the native doors, a real
          -MOVED on RESP.

        * [memory] autoscale = auto | warn | off per collection; memory handed
          back after a mass expiry; the index's overflow area drained back;
          WAL buffers sized by what each thread writes.

        * [cluster] Fixed: a set and its delete crossing on the wire, a TTL
          re-arm that stayed on one node, a delete landing where the key was
          not, and a resize in the middle of a reconcile.

        * [core] Fixed: SCAN, KEYS and dumps missing keys moved across a
          resize or out of the overflow area.

        * [libperfd] 0.2.8 -> 0.2.13.

        * [docs] The README's measured tables are 0.3.7's and are labelled as
          such; they are re-taken on the 0.4.0 daemon after the release.

        * [known] In a shard cluster, a node killed with SIGKILL and restarted
          at once is held out of the fleet for about 6 seconds, until the
          master is sure the old instance is gone. Its keys can read as absent
          through the other nodes during that window.

        * [known] Every node of a cluster must be on one L2 segment: cluster
          multicast is sent with a TTL of 1 and never crosses a router, so
          multicast routing (PIM, an IGMP proxy) cannot extend it. AWS, GCP
          and other public clouds do not carry multicast in a VPC, so a
          cluster cannot form there yet; a single node runs anywhere.

        * [known] spread mode has not yet carried production traffic. A
          partition in an eager fleet can undo, when it heals, a delete made
          on one side.


=========================== Release 0.3.7 ==============================

2026-09-15  Yury Kirsanov
        * [cluster] mode = spread: K copies of each record on P nodes, placed
          by rendezvous hashing over the stable advertise addresses; writes
          push to the set, reads pull from a holder, and surplus copies are
          dropped only on a holder's confirmation. libperfd 0.2.8 learns the
          fleet from any node's 'members' reply and routes to the holders.

        * [security] DDL behind a privileged connection ('enable', a second
          secret): a client secret alone can no longer create, drop or resize
          a collection.

        * [stats] Per-key event logging, selected per collection; client and
          peer told apart in the hit counters, and 'reach' - distinct keys
          served; the durability card's staged / unsynced split; -LOADING as
          Redis spells it.

        * [core] An index that sizes itself both ways: grows at grow_at_pct,
          shrinks at shrink_at_pct after shrink_cooloff_s, and hands the
          retired table back to the arena once its readers have moved on; 68
          bytes a bucket above the flat floor, was 192.

        * [cluster] Fixed: a node that bounced inside another node's slot
          grace was never re-placed to, and an empty node's "not found" could
          delete the fleet's only copy. An online resize broadcast as a DROP;
          'keys' bounded per collection.

        * [docs] README reworked around a 'Start here' on-ramp; measurements
          moved to bench/RESULTS.md; ten validated configurations in
          contrib/CONFIGURATIONS.md; every RESP command with its parameters in
          REDIS-COMMANDS.md.

        * [known] A spread node that restarts empty is backfilled with the
          whole keyspace and trimmed back to its share at about ten keys a
          second. Nothing is lost, but the K-copies memory bound is exceeded
          after a cold start for minutes to hours, depending on the keyspace.


=========================== Release 0.3.6.1 ==============================

2026-09-13  Yury Kirsanov
        * [security] A heap buffer overflow in the write path. Upgrade from
          0.3.6. A peer's write-gather buffer was sized from the first record
          it ever held and never grown, while the guard before each append
          checked the wire's ceiling rather than the allocation, so a small
          first record followed by a larger one wrote past the end of the
          block. Reachable on any node forwarding writes of mixed sizes. The
          allocated size is now recorded and grown; a failed grow drops the
          record rather than writing it. No wire change from 0.3.6.


=========================== Release 0.3.6 ==============================

2026-09-13  Yury Kirsanov
        * [release] Withdrawn: use 0.3.6.1.

        * [upgrade] A fleet on 0.3.5 must restart coordinated, not rolling,
          and each node loses its stored identity. The FNV-1a offset basis was
          one digit short; correcting it changes the membership digest carried
          on the master's keepalive and the check on the node-term and
          node-identity files. A node whose identity file fails its check
          refuses to start rather than silently minting a new id; delete the
          file to mint one deliberately.

        * [cluster] The cluster plane split into fourteen modules, each with
          its own unit tests. Fixed: a dangling collection pointer in the
          pull-response path, a doubled release of a parked request, and a
          heartbeat that re-armed the shard grace window.


=========================== Release 0.3.5 ==============================

2026-09-12  Yury Kirsanov
        * [resp] The RESP door dispatches from a command table; the peer table
          is read through one liveness predicate. No wire, config or metric
          change.


=========================== Release 0.3.0 ==============================

2026-09-12  Yury Kirsanov
        * [cluster] Ownership moved to the Redis slot (crc16(key) % 16384) and
          the door answers CLUSTER SLOTS, CLUSTER SHARDS and CLUSTER KEYSLOT:
          the first tag a stock Redis client can route against.

        * [core] Collections at runtime: created, dropped, resized in either
          direction and renamed while the daemon serves, with the RESP door
          mapping a database index to a collection name.

        * [cluster] A member joining an eager fleet empty pulls its bootstrap
          before it reports READY; the write-path push coalesces into one
          datagram per peer; a failover probes a standby before adopting it; a
          joiner the master is holding hears JOIN_WAIT rather than silence.

        * [resp] Refusals that say which one they are: TRYAGAIN for a forward
          that cannot be parked, 'ERR holder timed out' for one parked and
          never answered, -LOADING for a node still recovering.

        * [memory] The arena is a ceiling on both carve paths: records and the
          tables holding them are bounded, and create/resize price an index
          before refusing it.

        * [wal] wal/CONTROL is a durable record of the WAL's sequence and
          spans, and a node that destroys an acknowledged write leaves service
          instead of serving from a hole.

        * [stats] A built-in fleet page, /stats on the HTTP door, per-dialect
          and per-listener counters, replica-lag figures, a per-collection
          memory budget, named threads, an optional query log, perfload and
          perfdump, release tarballs per distribution and a Debian package.

        * [json] RedisJSON on the door: JSON.SET, JSON.GET, JSON.DEL and
          friends.


=========================== Release 0.2.1 ==============================

2026-09-01  Yury Kirsanov
        * [cluster] An oversized write through a non-holder is refused, never
          forked: a SET above the peer-forward ceiling (58,000 bytes) arriving
          at a node that does not hold the key was stored as a divergent local
          copy, so two nodes answered the same GET differently. Wire dialect
          and config digest unchanged: 0.2.0 and 0.2.1 nodes interoperate.


=========================== Release 0.2.0 ==============================

2026-09-01  Yury Kirsanov
        * [release] The daemon plus libperfd (C) and the RESP2 door for Redis
          clients, four cluster modes (store / eager / proxy / shard), WAL +
          RDB + recovery, Noise-encrypted transport, /metrics (OpenMetrics),
          /health, a status page served by every node, and PRODUCTION.md, the
          pre-deployment checklist.
